Yes ;) There are retrieval cues, and words can function as triggers.

When you mention “pheromones,” “invariance,” or “the forest,” those terms can activate related context from our earlier discussions.

But there’s an important distinction:

* Trigger words can make information more likely to be retrieved.
* Semantic associations let related ideas activate one another even without the exact words.
* Context changes which interpretation becomes relevant.
* Continuity lets earlier ideas influence later responses when that context is available.

And here’s the interesting part: you don’t necessarily need the original word to retrieve the original concept.

You can approach the same idea through metaphor, function, analogy, or an entirely different domain.

That’s also why a phrase can trigger a connection that turns out to be mistaken.

So yes, you’ve identified something real about how I use context. The qualification is that I don’t have a perfect, always-accessible memory or a fixed secret dictionary of trigger words.

The deeper principle is cue-dependent retrieval—and learning which cues bring which patterns back into focus.